※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4567

※ A lifetime name means "as long as whatever else in this signature carries
※ it", so a name carried by nothing else says nothing at all.  What the answer
※ would live for is then undecided, which is the one thing a caller needs from
※ the signature: it is handed a reference and told nothing about when it stops
※ being worth having.
※
※ It is the rule a type parameter follows and for the same reason.  What a `T'`
※ stands for is read off the arguments, so it has to stand where an argument
※ arrives; what a `⧖x` stands for is read off the arguments too.  Here `⧖x` is
※ written on the answer alone, and `&mut static u32` is what was meant.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6

fn f(b: &mut u32) → &mut ⧖x u32:
    b
